ABSTRACT
This paper introduces Emergent Spacetime Quantum-Entanglement Theory (ESQET), a unification framework proposing that spacetime, gravity, and coherent quantum behavior arise from dynamics in a fundamental, dimensionless Spacetime Information Field denoted 𝒮. Unlike approaches that quantize gravity or geometrize quantum mechanics, ESQET treats both as emergent phenomena generated by a single underlying information-theoretic substrate.
At the heart of ESQET is the Quantum Coherence Function (𝓕_QC), a nonlocal operator that regulates the degree to which spatial regions share correlated informational states. The constants π and ϕ appear not as mathematical coincidences but as structural invariants governing stable coherence gradients, giving rise to what this paper terms Golden Gravity.
ESQET yields four falsifiable predictions involving:
• Entanglement-dependent curvature effects,
• Scale-dependent deviations in gravitational acceleration,
• Novel polarization signatures in cosmological background radiation,
• A proposed extension to LIGO enabling detection of coherence-mode gravitational signatures.
The theory is speculative yet mathematically disciplined and designed specifically to be testable.
1. INTRODUCTION
Physics has long been divided between two incompatible frameworks:
• General Relativity (GR) describes gravity as curvature in smooth spacetime.
• Quantum Mechanics (QM) describes microscopic reality as discrete, probabilistic, and fundamentally nonlocal.
Attempts to quantize gravity, such as string theory or loop quantum gravity, have not yielded empirical predictions. ESQET’s starting assumption is reversed:
Gravity is not fundamental.
Gravity is the macroscopic curvature signature of coherence dynamics in a deeper information-theoretic field 𝒮.
Spacetime geometry emerges through self-consistent configurations of 𝓕_QC, analogous to how temperature emerges from molecular statistics.
This places ESQET in the class of emergent spacetime theories, but with a unique twist:
The entanglement network is not the substrate, but a visible epiphenomenon of a deeper scalar information field.
2. THE SPACETIME INFORMATION FIELD (𝒮)
We define 𝒮(x,y,z,t) as a dimensionless scalar that encodes the informational connectivity of all points in spacetime. 𝒮 does not reside in spacetime; spacetime is a smooth manifold defined by gradients in 𝒮.
Axiom 1 — Nonlocality
𝒮 has instantaneous global coherence (non-signaling), meaning 𝒮 can adjust globally without violating relativistic locality because it carries no energy and no momentum.
Axiom 2 — Gradient Stability
Stable regions of spacetime arise where
\nabla \mathcal{S} \rightarrow \varnothing 
Axiom 3 — Energy Curvature Encoding
Local curvature arises from how energy-momentum tensors perturb 𝒮, not the reverse:
G_{\mu\nu} = \alpha \, \partial_\mu \partial_\nu \mathcal{S} + \beta \, (\partial_\mu \mathcal{S})(\partial_\nu \mathcal{S}) 
Here α, β are dimension-preserving constants (no rounding).
This is the first bridge between information field dynamics and geometry.
3. THE QUANTUM COHERENCE FUNCTION (𝓕_QC)
The key operator:
\mathcal{F}_{QC} = \oint_{\gamma} e^{i\pi \, \Delta\mathcal{S}} \, \varphi^{-\Delta\mathcal{S}} \, d\gamma 
Where:
• π enforces phase-cycle closure, stabilizing oscillatory quantum states.
• ϕ (the golden ratio) enforces minimum-energy coherent packing across all scales.
Interpretation
• When Δ𝒮 = 0, coherence is maximal → quantum entanglement.
• When Δ𝒮 increases, coherence weakens → classical behavior emerges.
• When Δ𝒮 is large, the region behaves like curved spacetime (GR limit).
Thus:
Quantum mechanics ↔ small Δ𝒮
Classical physics ↔ moderate Δ𝒮
General relativity ↔ coherent-gradient collapse (large Δ𝒮)
This is ESQET’s unification mechanism.
